## Summary

Two fixes to how recorded runs relate to the rest of the world.

**1. Ghosts are transparent to the player only.** They were excluded from every collision set, so a
ghost walked through the crate it had originally shoved. Recorded bodies are now solids for objects
(never for the live player), and their per-tick displacement is applied to whatever they touch before
box physics runs:

```ts
private applyGhostMotion(target) {
  for (const run of runs) {
    const pr = playerRect(run.states[now]), nr = playerRect(run.states[target]);
    const dx = nr.x - pr.x, dy = nr.y - pr.y;
    for (const box of boxes) {
      if (restingOn(box, pr))          moveX/moveY(box, dx, dy);   // crate rides the ghost
      else if (overlaps(nr, box))      moveX(box, exitAmount(dx));  // ghost shoves it aside
    }
  }
}
stepBoxesForward(target) {
  applyGhostMotion(target);
  const others = [...otherBoxSolids(box), ...ghostSolidsAt(target)];  // crates land on ghosts
  ...
}
```

Paradox detection is unchanged and now means something sharper: a crate only contradicts history when
the ghost *can't* shove it clear (pinned against geometry), rather than on any overlap.

**2. Ghost flicker at device pads.** `stepPlayerFrozen` wrote the live position into
`current.states[now]` every frame while a chronoporter/anachroverter held the timeline. That single
tick of the run then held a pad-exit position instead of its historical one, so whenever time passed
back over it the ghost snapped to the device and back. Freezing now records nothing, and stepping off
a pausing pad always calls `splitRun()` — the frozen movement starts a new worldline instead of
overwriting the old one's last tick. `deviceEntryTime`/`deviceEntryDir`/`timelineTouched` existed only
to decide whether to split, and are gone.

`npm run check` gains: the ghost re-pushes the crate along the recorded path after a scrub, the frozen
tick keeps its recorded state, and the end-to-end route still completes with 0 paradox ticks.


